issue_context.body:
I'm implementing DMX512 input on an F4.

Reading it's done at 250000 bauds, the Break and the Mark After Break it's at 100000 so it's common practice to detect a framing error instead of switching baudrate or edge detection. 

I have an interrupt handler for the UART IRQ, inside it I have this piece of code: 

    while (uart_irq_update(dev) && uart_irq_is_pending(dev)) {
       if (uart_irq_rx_ready(dev)) {   
            int err = uart_err_check(dev);
            if (err > 0)
            {
                if(err == UART_ERROR_OVERRUN ){
                    printk("[UART0_RXTX] UART_ERROR_OVERRUN \n");
                }
                if(err == UART_ERROR_PARITY  ){
                    printk("[UART0_RXTX] UART_ERROR_PARITY \n ");
                }
                if(err == UART_ERROR_FRAMING ){
                    printk("[UART0_RXTX] UART_ERROR_FRAMING \n ");
                }
                if(err == UART_BREAK ){
                    printk("[UART0_RXTX] UART_BREAK \n");
                }
                if(err == UART_ERROR_COLLISION ){
                    printk("[UART0_RXTX] UART_ERROR_COLLISION \n");
                }
            }
            int ret = uart_fifo_read(dev,&_rx_buffer,1);
            if (ret > 0)
            {
                printk("read data %d\n"_rx_buffer);
            }
            if (ret < 0)
            {
                printk("error reading data\n");
            }
            else{
                printk("no data\n");   
            }
        }

I should be able to detect the Break or the data but if I detect the Break , uart_fifo_read always returns 0,  this doesn't happen if I try to not detect the break.

I'm expecting to be able to check the error flags and be able to read from the buffer if no error has occured.

comments:
Seems that Line Break Detection is missing from `uart_stm32_err_check`;
I'm not sure about the reason why it is missing but we can try to fix this.

I can't test this myself right now, but maybe you can have a try to add the following, in `drivers/uart_stm32.c`, in function ``, just after the framing error detection and before clearing errors ([here](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/682f261c95ef8fbbd9f587e36f466f9382611454/drivers/serial/uart_stm32.c#L592)):
```
	if (LL_USART_IsActiveFlag_LBD(UartInstance)) {
		err |= UART_BREAK;
	}

	if (err & UART_BREAK) {
		LL_USART_ClearFlag_LBD(UartInstance);
	}
```

Can you try this and see if fixing your point ?

Please note that by default, line break detection is set for 10-bit break. You'll need to make some changes for 11-bit break detection.
